"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"Cwlr9bIj21"},{"type":"text","value":" falls inside the confidence interval, do not reject H~o~.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"bW4bM6g7Pz"}],"key":"ZLiiJQVgF3"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"UxW8gcCVZ6"}],"key":"K0myshFr2g"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"We'll use example 2, in which the alternative was two-sided.\n\nRecall that we want to check whether a medication conforms to a target concentration of a chemical ingredient by testing\n\n$H_{0}:\\mu=250$\n\n$H_{a}:\\mu\\neq250$\n\nWe assume that $\\sigma=12$, and in a sample of size n = 100 we obtained a sample mean of $\\bar{x}=247$.\n\nA 95% confidence interval for μ is $\\bar{x}\\pm2\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}=247\\pm2\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{100}}=247\\pm2.4=\\left(244.6, 249.4\\right)$.\n\nSince the interval does not contain 250, we reject H~o~ and conclude that the alternative is true: the population mean concentration differs from 250.\n\n```{figure} images/image354.gif\n:alt: A number line showing the 95% confidence interval, which is (244.6, 249.4). 250 is also marked on the number line, and it isn&apos;t within the interval, so we can be confident that μ isn&apos;t 250. In other words, we can reject the hypothesis H_0:μ = 250\n```","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"LDJPWnTVYn"}],"key":"D00BfnjfX0"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Did I Get This?","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"weyYch34WY"}],"identifier":"did-i-get-this","label":"Did I Get This?","html_id":"did-i-get-this","implicit":true,"key":"GDQuD0lzfr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"One of the following is an actual Minitab output; the others were edited to be incorrect.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"ZtaRRloN08"}],"key":"hQaZY4A1mW"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/image392-a474b50ba940a07579926448706d873d.gif","alt":"Output: One-Sample Z: Test of mu=264 vs not = 264 The assumed standard deviation = 16 N: 35 Mean: 260.000 SE Mean: 2.704 95% CI: (254.699, 265.301) Z: -2.22 P: 0.047","key":"fTLq9PJAF6","urlSource":"images/image392.gif"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"Mc4UEGm4Q2"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/image393-10837ea0d827e5b237d8cbdd0103c24d.gif","alt":"Output: One-Sample Z: Test of mu=267 vs not = 267 The assumed standard deviation = 16 N: 35 Mean: 260.000 SE Mean: 2.704 95% CI: (254.699, 265.301) Z: -2.22 P: 0.087","key":"P5d0cTJJWL","urlSource":"images/image393.gif"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"Mknnvf0NOS"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/image394-f36ea5407b8095924058a558a6f2e467.gif","alt":"Output: One-Sample Z: Test of mu=266 vs not = 266 The assumed standard deviation = 16 N: 35 Mean: 260.000 SE Mean: 2.704 95% CI: (254.699, 265.301) Z: -2.22 P: 0.027","key":"ok4DR3OHpV","urlSource":"images/image394.gif"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"MkSGNZolSQ"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"dPn7lQYgeJ"}],"key":"f4qCZWoG9c"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Sectionnest**\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"ExEKFPjnmD"}],"key":"RqTNEXa9jS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Did You Get It?","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"h0fqNAi9FO"}],"key":"tJVf9aJuLS"},{"type":"text","value":" If so, then go ahead and move on. If not, then click the link below for some additional practice.","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"sYv2zMV2uk"}],"key":"alUYeTsIJs"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"uvYmeWEOZa"}],"key":"uECGdSxANf"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Sectionnest**\n\nHypothesis Testing for the Population Mean (Extra Problem)\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":66,"column":1}},"key":"fuCZvt7Btq"}],"key":"rXRMpu0K65"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":69,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":69,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"key":"uSM77Bz3cs"}],"identifier":"comment","label":"Comment","html_id":"comment","implicit":true,"key":"pDI9CPOc3S"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Beyond using the confidence interval as a quick way to carry out the two-sided test, the confidence interval can provide insight into the actual value of the population mean if H~o~ is rejected. In the concentration level example, H~o~ was rejected, and all we could conclude about the mean concentration level of the entire shipment, μ, was that it was not 250. The 95% confidence interval for μ (244.6, 249.4) gives us an idea of what plausible values for μ would be. In particular, we can conclude that since the confidence interval lies below 250, at least a large portion of the shipment contains medication that is ineffective.","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"GyXQps0XUP"}],"key":"wLrYFHevQw"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"de5Qu4Jjz6"}],"key":"QWcEAWtoxL"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Learn By Doing**\n\nHypothesis Testing for the Population Mean\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"oLMub49z6z"}],"key":"Eg22DhqvzW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you feel that you understand this concept?","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"key":"wwPeRqCwqg"}],"key":"yvP0SRgajL"},{"type":"text","value":" If so, then go ahead and move on. If not, then click the link below for some additional practice.","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"key":"rWtYM4WiYp"}],"key":"PF1RCDODSi"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"dqjQOI1Mhn"}],"key":"w1HgFOimu4"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Learn By Doing**\n\nHypothesis Testing for the Population Mean (Extra Problems)\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"v31RHsgW8f"}],"key":"dkmDQxo9K2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We are done with the case where the population standard deviation, σ, is known. We now move on to the more common case where σ is unknown.","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"c3hRJuOVoo"}],"key":"pc761KjWsF"}],"key":"irk6rMJ8gb"}],"key":"VXQjReazpj"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Hypothesis Testing for the Population Mean (4 of 9)","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-4-o","group":"Section 15: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"Hypothesis Testing for the Population Mean (6 of 9)","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-6-o","group":"Section 15: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}